WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:10.000
It was supposed to be a public service announcement promoting the dangers of using drugs.

00:00:10.000 --> 00:00:15.000
Instead, the ad unwittingly came off as promoting illicit substances.

00:00:15.000 --> 00:00:18.000
I am Fyengie, help me.

00:00:18.000 --> 00:00:25.000
The PSA from Hong Kong's Correctional Services Department featured an AI-generated K-pop girl group called Obsession.

00:00:25.000 --> 00:00:30.600
Each member represented a different drug, who later morphed into ugly old man at the end of the video.

00:00:31.180 --> 00:00:37.680
But the video was quickly taken down after netizens said it appeared to promote rather than discourage drug use.

00:00:41.680 --> 00:00:45.620
Authorities published another version that erroneously said

00:00:45.620 --> 00:00:53.000
this version was also removed on the evening of June 27th.

00:00:53.000 --> 00:00:58.000
Not surprisingly, the unwanted publicity gained even more traction.

00:00:58.000 --> 00:01:08.000
Posts of the virtual band members' selfies, sticker packs, image sets, advertisements, fan pages and song parodies have been popping up all over social media.

00:01:13.000 --> 00:01:19.000
The CSD later issued an apology, saying the in-house production did not incur additional public funds.

00:01:19.000 --> 00:01:23.300
It's not the first time Hong Kong's anti-drug campaign has gone viral.

00:01:23.300 --> 00:01:28.380
In March, a similar incident happened at an MTR station after a banner appealed to read

00:01:28.380 --> 00:01:32.380
Stand firm together and take drugs when viewed from certain angles.

00:01:32.380 --> 00:01:35.960
It was part of a campaign featuring Canto Pop star Aaron Kwok.

00:01:35.960 --> 00:01:40.440
It too became a meme after it featured a clunky dance and slogan.

00:01:40.440 --> 00:01:43.340
For more on the story, check out sdmp.com
